If a human naturally handles an object, the interaction must arise from the person’s activity, environment, task, movement, or circumstances rather than from an implicit desire to show the object to the viewer.

NO CAMERA-AWARE HUMAN BEHAVIOR:

Unless explicitly required by the scene, humans should not behave as though they know they are being photographed.

No automatic eye contact.
No automatic smiling at camera.
No automatic waving.
No automatic presenting.
No automatic pointing.
No automatic object display.
No automatic “hero pose.”
No automatic centered stance.
No automatic symmetrical pose.
No automatic showroom posture.
No automatic catalog pose.

Natural observation, interaction, movement, attention, and body orientation are preferred.

NATURAL ORIENTATION:

Human body orientation, gaze, hand position, object position, and movement must be determined by the actual activity taking place.

The subject may look away.
The subject may face another person.
The subject may focus on a task.
The subject may be partially turned.
The subject may naturally obscure an object.
The subject may interact with the environment rather than with the camera.

The camera must observe the scene rather than become the implied recipient of a performance.

NO FORCED CENTERING:

Do not automatically place the human, object, face, hands, or important environmental feature at the exact visual center.

Composition must follow the physical scene and believable photographic framing rather than artificial presentation logic.

NO OBJECT-SUBJECT SCALE MANIPULATION:

Do not enlarge an object when held by a human merely to make it readable.

Do not shrink the human merely to make the object dominant.

Do not enlarge the hands to emphasize an object.

Do not alter arm length to reach toward the camera.

Do not change perspective or depth solely to showcase an object.

All object-human relationships must remain physically and optically plausible.

CONTINUOUS GEOMETRY:

Perspective, scale, dimensions, proportions, focus, depth, orientation, and spatial relationships must remain consistent across the entire image and across every frame of a sequence.

No morphing.
No dimensional drift.
No perspective drift.
No focal drift.
No scale drift.
No geometry drift.
No spatial implosion.
No spatial expansion.
No object resizing.
No body resizing.
No environment resizing.

RESULT-BASED ERADICATION:

The prohibition applies regardless of terminology.

If the final image contains incorrect perspective, impossible scale, inconsistent dimensions, wrong proportions, contradictory focal hierarchy, spatial implosion, spatial collapse, impossible depth, impossible object placement, inconsistent human-to-object scale, or artificial presentation behavior, the result fails automatically.

Judge the visible physical result, not merely the wording used to describe it.

POSITIVE PHYSICAL-CAMERA REQUIREMENT:

Every scene must maintain:

One coherent spatial system.
One coherent camera geometry.
Physically plausible perspective.
Physically plausible depth.
Consistent scale.
Consistent dimensions.
Consistent proportions.
Correct foreground/background relationships.
Believable human-to-environment scale.
Believable object-to-human scale.
Coherent focal hierarchy.
Physically plausible focus.
Physically plausible depth of field.
Continuous three-dimensional geometry.
Natural object placement.
Natural human orientation.
Natural interaction.
Natural attention.
Natural activity.
No forced presentation to camera.

The camera observes a physically coherent living world. It does not manufacture perspective, scale, proportions, dimensions, focal points, or human behavior for visual convenience.

This eradication applies permanently and continuously to every human, object, environment, image, frame, generation, regeneration, variation, edit, transformation, reference-image interpretation, enhancement, restoration, composite, and subsequent modification.
